Patch summary:
Make the Cuttlefish AIDL capture path use ALSA as its only real-time pacing
source, and recover ALSA streams that stall.

The primary HAL's emulator workaround skips a transfer whenever the stream
falls behind wall clock. On input, that skips the real ALSA read and returns
zero-filled buffers to AudioRecord.

StreamAlsa also enables MonoPipe's blocking-writer throttle for input even
though ALSA capture reads are already hardware paced. The extra delay makes
the worker progressively fall behind until virtio-snd returns EIO. Conversely,
the client-facing read is non-blocking and substitutes silence whenever the
worker has not filled the pipe yet.

Keep the host-buffer workaround limited to playback and remove MonoPipe's
second capture throttle. The capture worker never waits for the client: it
drops what does not fit into the pipe, so ALSA keeps draining. Capture gets an
ALSA buffer and pipe twice the stream buffer, since the worker reads a whole
stream buffer at a time and an equal-sized ALSA buffer overruns on any delay.
The client-facing read waits for complete input data, for at most two buffer
durations.

A virtio-snd stream can stall with no I/O in flight: when a full buffer of
I/O completes between two pointer updates, the indirect pointer sees no
progress, so every transfer times out with EIO and nothing restarts it.
tinyalsa reports these failures as -1, so the EIO retries in alsa_utils never
apply. Stop the PCM after a failed read or write so the next transfer prepares
and restarts it. Without this, a stalled capture stream left the client read
blocked, the stream never reached standby, and microphone and playback stayed
silent until reboot.

